  "schoolyard": 1,
  "schoolyard_use_notes": "This tree is great for learning about leaf morphology and variation, ethnobotany, and and conservation biology. The waxy  leaves of this tree offer an interesting introduction to different surface textures of leaves. The leaves also vary widely in shape within one tree and among many trees; some are more deeply lobed while others may be entire.  The acorns are an important food resource for indigenous people and are still processed for cooking, baking,  and candies. The acorns and acorn cupules are also great materials for natural art projects. The tree is listed as endangered on the US Red List for Oaks as they have suffered from habitat loss and struggled to regenerate and reproduce naturally due to intense grazing, soil compaction, and habitat fragmentation.\n",
